WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 98: The Salem Witch Trials of 1692: Hysteria and Spectral Evidence in Massachusetts Bay

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.920
[Rebecca] Welcome back to most fascinating stories.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.200
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:21.520
[Rebecca] Today, we step into the bleak, fear-haunted winter of 1692 in Massachusetts Bay Colony to confront one of the darkest, most terrifying, and enduringly cautionary chapters in American history, the Salem Witch Trials.

4
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:37.760
[Rebecca] In an isolated Puritan farming village beset by Indian wars, smallpox epidemics, and deep factional hatred, a group of young girls began displaying bizarre contortions, screaming in agony, and claiming to be tormented by invisible specters sent by the devil.

5
00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:50.640
[Rebecca] What followed was a deadly epidemic of paranoia and judicial hysteria that swept across New England, claiming the lives of 20 innocent men and women before the voice of reason finally shattered the spectral delusion.

6
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.320
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full harrowing chronicle.

7
00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:05.600
[George] In the winter of 1691 and 1692, the Massachusetts Bay colony was gripped by an overwhelming sense of existential dread.

8
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:20.400
[George] The Puritan settlers who had crossed the Atlantic to build a pious city upon a hill, believed with absolute theological certainty that they inhabited a dual universe where the invisible spiritual realm constantly waged war against the physical world.

9
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.640
[George] To the Puritan mind, Satan was not an abstract metaphor.

10
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:35.680
[George] He was a living, cunning adversary who roamed the primeval New England wilderness, seeking to subvert the holy commonwealth through human agents, witches who signed his black book in blood.

11
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.880
[George] This pervasive spiritual anxiety was compounded by brutal physical realities.

12
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:53.680
[George] The colony was reeling from the bloody trauma of King Philip's War and King William's war, with frontier settlements repeatedly burned and refugees flooding coastal towns with horrific tales of scalpings and massacres.

13
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.160
[George] A recent smallpox epidemic had decimated entire congregations.

14
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:06.280
[George] Winters were brutally cold, and the revocation of the Massachusetts Charter by the English Crown had stripped the colonists of clear legal government.

15
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.360
[George] The entire society felt vulnerable, anxious, and ripe for an explosion of scapegoating.

16
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:23.880
[George] In Salem Village, a contentious, impoverished agricultural parish located five miles inland from the prosperous mercantile seaport of Salem town, tensions were particularly toxic.

17
00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:40.840
[George] The village was deeply fractured by bitter land disputes, family feuds, and a raging controversy surrounding its newly appointed, fire and brimstone minister, the Reverend Samuel Parris, whose obsessive sermons warned constantly that the devil was infiltrating his church.

18
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:49.080
[George] Puritan theology saw the wilderness as Satan's primeval empire, waiting to overwhelm their fragile holy commonwealth at any moment.

19
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:56.200
[George] In January 1692, inside the drafty parsonage of Reverend Paris, the nightmare began.

20
00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:07.160
[George] Paris's nine-year-old daughter, Betty, and his eleven-year-old orphaned niece, Abigail Williams, began exhibiting bizarre, disturbing behaviors that terrified the household.

21
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:19.960
[George] The girls threw themselves onto the floor, barked like dogs, contorted their limbs into unnatural angles, crawled under chairs, and screamed that invisible needles and red-hot pins were piercing their flesh.

22
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:25.160
[George] Soon, several other village girls, including twelve year old Anne Putnam J.

23
00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:25.480
[George] R.

24
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.200
[George] and seventeen year old Mercy Lewis, succumbed to identical fits.

25
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:38.680
[George] Village physician William Griggs examined the children, but finding no natural disease, offered a terrifying theological diagnosis.

26
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.360
[George] The girls were bewitched, afflicted by the evil hand.

27
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:50.160
[George] Desperate to uncover who was tormenting the children, Reverend Paris and local magistrates pressed the weeping girls to name their tormentors.

28
00:03:50.400 --> 00:04:00.080
[George] In late February, the afflicted girls leveled their first three accusations, strategically targeting women on the vulnerable margins of Puritan society.

29
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:08.880
[George] Tituba, an enslaved indigenous or Caribbean woman in Paris's household who had reportedly shared folk divination rituals with the girls.

30
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:24.400
[George] Sarah Good, an impoverished, pipe-smoking beggar known for muttering sullen curses when denied food, and Sarah Osborne, an elderly bedridden widow who had scandalized the community by avoiding church attendance and living with an indentured servant.

31
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:33.040
[George] Reverend Paris's frantic interrogation of the afflicted children turned private family distress into a matter of colonial state security.

32
00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:45.440
[George] On March 1, 1692, local magistrates John Hathorne and Jonathan Corwin arrived in Salem Village to conduct formal preliminary examinations inside the crowded village meeting house.

33
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.560
[George] The atmosphere was charged with religious hysteria.

34
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:56.720
[George] When Sarah Good and Sarah Osborne were brought forward, they vigorously protested their innocence, denying any pact with the devil.

35
00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:09.600
[George] But every time the accused women shifted their eyes, the afflicted girls in the front pews threw themselves into wild convulsions, shrieking that the women's invisible specters were biting, choking, and pinching them.

36
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:15.600
[George] Then came the turning point that transformed a localized incident into an unstoppable wildfire.

37
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.640
[George] The testimony of the enslaved woman Tituba.

38
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:27.760
[George] Terrified by beatings from Reverend Paris, Tituba delivered a dramatic, mesmerizing confession that confirmed the Puritan's worst apocalyptic fears.

39
00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:34.360
[George] She declared that the devil had indeed visited her in the shape of a black dog and a red cat, demanding that she serve him.

40
00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:45.720
[George] She testified that she had seen a tall man from Boston with a black book containing nine names written in blood, and that a grand coven of witches was actively plotting the destruction of Salem Village.

41
00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:49.240
[George] Tatuba's confession electrified the community with terror.

42
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:55.800
[George] It validated the magistrates' suspicions and established that a vast, secret conspiracy was at work.

43
00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.240
[George] The hunt was no longer confined to social outcasts.

44
00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:08.200
[George] Within weeks, the afflicted girls began pointing their fingers at pious, respected church members, prosperous merchants, and even children.

45
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:16.760
[George] Tituba's vivid descriptions of midnight sabbats and demonic familiars confirmed the worst apocalyptic nightmares of the Puritan magistrates.

46
00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:28.200
[George] By May 1692, over 100 citizens were crammed into squalid, disease-ridden jails in Salem, Boston, and Ipswich, shackled in heavy iron chains.

47
00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:41.800
[George] Newly arrived royal governor Sir William Phipps established a special tribunal, the court of Oyer and Terminor, meaning to hear and determine, presided over by the stern, unforgiving Chief Justice William Staughton.

48
00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:47.160
[George] The fatal legal foundation of the Salem trials was the admission of spectral evidence.

49
00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:59.960
[George] Under this bizarre legal doctrine, the court accepted as verified physical testimony the claim that an accused person's invisible spirit or specter could leave their body and afflict victims miles away.

50
00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:15.200
[George] Chief Justice Staughton and the magistrates argued that God would never permit the devil to assume the shape of an innocent person, meaning that if an afflicted girl claimed to see John Proctor's specter sitting on a beam or choking her, Proctor was legally guilty of witchcraft.

51
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.200
[George] This created an impossible, Kafka esque trap for the accused.

52
00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:26.480
[George] If a defendant vigorously protested their innocence, they were viewed as hardened agents of Satan refusing to repent.

53
00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:33.040
[George] If they confessed to witchcraft and begged for mercy, they were spared execution to name other conspirators.

54
00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:37.520
[George] Truth was punished with death, while perjury was rewarded with life.

55
00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:42.800
[George] On June 10, 1692, the court carried out its first execution.

56
00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:52.400
[George] Bridget Bishop, a tavern keeper known for wearing a provocative scarlet bodice, was marched to Gallows Hill and hanged from an oak tree before a silent crowd.

57
00:07:52.400 --> 00:08:00.960
[George] The fatal admission of spectral evidence effectively eliminated all legal defense, reducing court proceedings to state-sanctioned theater.

58
00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:11.040
[George] As summer dragged on, the hangings multiplied on Gallows Hill, but public unease began to curdle into revulsion as respected saints of the church were condemned.

59
00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:24.880
[George] Among them was seventy-one-year-old Rebecca Nurse, a frail, deeply devout grandmother and pillars of Salem Church whose spotless character was attested to by thirty-nine neighbors who risked their own lives to sign a petition of support.

60
00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:35.920
[George] The jury initially found Rebecca not guilty, but when the afflicted girls shrieked in protest, Chief Justice Staughton sent the jury back to reconsider until they returned a guilty verdict.

61
00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.880
[George] Rebecca was hanged on July 19, dying with serene Christian dignity.

62
00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:48.880
[George] In August, five more victims were hanged, including the Reverend George Burroughs, former minister of Salem Village.

63
00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] Standing at the foot of the ladder with the noose around his neck, Burroughs delivered a flawless, unhesitating recitation of the Lord's Prayer, a feat Puritans believed impossible for a witch to perform.

64
00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:14.680
[George] The spectators were so moved that they began demanding his release, but the influential Boston minister Cotton Mather rode up on horseback and pacified the crowd, insisting that the devil often transformed himself into an angel of light.

65
00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:20.840
[George] The most horrific spectacle occurred on September 19 in the case of 81-year-old Giles Corey.

66
00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:30.280
[George] Knowing that a conviction would result in the forfeiture of his estate, disinheriting his sons, Corey bravely refused to enter a plea of guilty or not guilty.

67
00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:39.400
[George] Under ancient English law, he was subjected to the barbaric punishment of pain forte et dur, pressed beneath heavy boulders on the open field.

68
00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:45.720
[George] For two agonizing days, Corey lay on his back in the dirt as more stones were piled upon his chest.

69
00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:56.760
[George] Whenever the sheriff demanded a plea, the old man replied only, More weight, he died of asphyxiation without uttering a word, preserving his property for his children.

70
00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:05.560
[George] Giles Corey's legendary refusal to speak demonstrated extraordinary moral courage against an unjust and fanatical judicial apparatus.

71
00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:20.200
[George] By late September 1692, nineteen innocent men and women had been hanged on Gallows Hill, Giles Corey had been pressed to death, and at least five others, including an infant child, had perished in frozen jail cells.

72
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.960
[George] But the madness was beginning to collapse under its own weight.

73
00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:39.800
[George] The afflicted girls, intoxicated by absolute power, grew reckless, accusing prominent figures including the president of Harvard College, the wife of Boston minister Increase Mather, and even Lady Mary Phipps, the wife of the royal governor himself.

74
00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] Influential ministers led by Increase Mather began speaking out with moral urgency.

75
00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:56.880
[George] In his landmark treatise Cases of Conscience Concerning Evil Spirits, Mather forcefully attacked the validity of spectral evidence, penning the immortal principle of Anglo American justice.

76
00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:03.120
[George] It were better that ten suspected witches should escape than that one innocent person should be condemned.

77
00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:11.440
[George] Recognizing that the trials were tearing Massachusetts society apart, Governor Phipps took decisive action in October 1692.

78
00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:20.080
[George] He abruptly dissolved the court of Oyer and Terminer, prohibited the use of spectral evidence, and ordered all pending prosecutions halted.

79
00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:26.800
[George] In May 1693, Phipps issued a general pardon, liberating the remaining prisoners from jail.

80
00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:30.160
[George] The deadly spell of hysteria was finally broken.

81
00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:40.000
[George] Increase Mathers' principled condemnation of spectral evidence restored legal sanity, establishing foundational protections for Western criminal jurisprudence.

82
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:46.880
[George] In the years that followed, a deep and agonizing wave of collective guilt washed over the people of Massachusetts.

83
00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:54.720
[George] In 1697, the general court declared an official day of public fasting and prayer for the sins committed at Salem.

84
00:11:54.720 --> 00:12:09.520
[George] In a dramatic moment of public atonement, trial judge Samuel Sewell stood before his Boston congregation with head bowed while his minister read his public confession, begging forgiveness from God and his fellow citizens for his terrible errors.

85
00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:24.880
[George] In 1706, Anne Putnam J.R., the most prolific of the afflicted girls, stood before the Salem Village Church to deliver a tearful public apology, confessing that she had been deluded by Satan and had falsely accused innocent neighbors.

86
00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:35.960
[George] In 1711, the Massachusetts legislature formally reversed the attainders of the victims, restoring their good names and paying financial restitution to their surviving children.

87
00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:46.680
[George] Today, the Salem witch trials stand as the ultimate American parable of the lethal danger of mass hysteria, religious extremism, and the erosion of due process.

88
00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:59.000
[George] The lessons of Salem, the necessity of confronting accusers, the presumption of innocence, and the prohibition of fabricated evidence became bedrock foundations of the United States Bill of Rights.

89
00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:15.080
[George] In modern Salem, a quiet, contemplative memorial garden stands beside Old Burying Point, where twenty granite benches bearing the names and execution dates of the innocent victims remind visitors of the sacred fragility of justice in a world governed by fear.

90
00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:24.280
[George] The quiet memorial park in Salem ensures that the terrible lessons of state-sponsored hysteria will never be forgotten by future generations.

91
00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:34.280
[Rebecca] The tragic lessons of Salem, the sacred necessity of due process, and the presumption of innocence became cornerstone principles of American justice.

92
00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:35.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

93
00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:50.600
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we walk the cobblestones of Boston's North End for the surreal disaster of the Great Molasses Flood.

